+# design-findings-memory.yaml -- Layer 2 design-grounding of the 'memory' category, PR #671 (issue #656), base main.
+# Playbook: review/memory, read from ~/.garura/phoenix-memory/knowledge/review/memory.md (Garura memory has no copy).
+# Every principle below is read from the base ref (git show main:<path>) or a committed decision record.
+# Not used as grounding: anything this branch adds or rewrites (the 656 plan, the PR body, the self-review, the scrubbed text itself),
+# and Phoenix memory's copy of the changed files (it was seeded after the scrub, so it carries the branch's own result).
+category: memory
+pr: 671
+issue: 656
+base: main
+scope: 41 changed paths (36 knowledge shelf files, 5 standards files) under core/components/memory/
+severity_basis: >
+  Buckets from core/components/memory/standards/rules/pr.md on main (P1 blocker; P2 major; P3 minor: missing docs, naming;
+  P4 nit: style). The table rows there are mechanical code rules and none targets memory prose, so each class below is chosen
+  from the bucket definitions. Prose is never a code defect (pr.md, "Prose is never a code/data defect (#454)").
+
+principles_grounded:
+  - id: G1
+    principle: >
+      A shelf file follows the template; its provenance is "seeded | learned from {product / issue}" and references an issue or
+      work context. The template names no person.
+    sources:
+      - "main:core/components/memory/knowledge/_TEMPLATE.md:15 (provenance: seeded | learned:product-or-issue) and :40 (## Provenance)"
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/templates/knowledge-file.md:86 (Provenance references an issue or work context)"
+  - id: G2
+    principle: >
+      The knowledge base is a bank of empirical learnings, each answering a conditional question; the selection facets
+      (id, title, conditions, evolve_when) and the Conditions / Recommendation prose are the durable content, and the real
+      risk is content quality and contradiction.
+    sources:
+      - "main:core/components/memory/knowledge/_DESIGN.md:1-8 (empirical learnings bank), :26 (template sections), :60-62 (the real risk)"
+  - id: G3
+    principle: >
+      Where a standard defines a field as a person, the field holds a person. Decision and approval fields name who decided or
+      who gave the thing; an accepted ADR must name who accepted it.
+    sources:
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/templates/adr.md:28 (accepted_by = The PERSON who accepted it) and :70 (**Decided by:** <person>)"
+      - "main:core/components/skills/author-adr/scripts/adr.py:147-148 and :190-191 (an accepted record names who accepted it)"
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/schemas/product-os/ontology.md:117 (Given by = The git user who ran the reading play, required)"
+      - "main:core/components/plays/intent/scripts/capture_source.py:107-108 (given-by must be the git user running the play) and persist_intent.py:380 (writes it into the page)"
+      - "main:docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md:6 (Decided by: Kapil Viren Ahuja) and docs/adr/036 header (same); both ADRs attribute quoted answers by name"
+  - id: G4
+    principle: >
+      A worked example in a standard is a real, filled-in instance written the way the reader will see it; a person-facing kind
+      is written in plain words with a plain example.
+    sources:
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/templates/product-ontology.md:60-62 (Example: a real, filled-in instance, written the way the reader will see it)"
+      - "main:core/components/skills/build-product-ontology/SKILL.md:125 (Readable by its reader ... a plain example)"
+  - id: G5
+    principle: >
+      One name carries one meaning, and terms are chosen so they are never confused. The framework's word for the human who
+      decides is "person".
+    sources:
+      - "main:docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md section 1 (the project tier is called Garura memory, not project memory or LTM, so it is never confused)"
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/schemas/product-os/grounding/lens/quality.md:15-17 and schemas/product-os/lens/quality-gates.yaml:21 (owner: machine | human is a defined field)"
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/rules/epics.md:13 (product owner) and knowledge/domains/collaboration.md:58 (owner shares to named people): two more meanings of owner"
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/rules/pr.md (P3 bucket names 'naming')"
+  - id: G6
+    principle: >
+      A change to the ontology is logged: bump version, set updated, add one dated Log line saying what changed and why. The Log
+      line records what changed and why, not who. Every change to Phoenix memory is a commit with history.
+    sources:
+      - "main:core/components/skills/build-product-ontology/SKILL.md:99-100 and :124"
+      - "main:core/components/memory/standards/templates/product-ontology.md:71 (## Log: {what changed in the ontology, and why})"
+      - "main:docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md:48 and :121 (every memory update is a commit, with history, review and versions)"
+  - id: G7
+    principle: >
+      Everything in core/components/memory/ seeds Phoenix memory (ADR 035 Affects and section 1). No committed source says what
+      may be rewritten at the seed point.
+    sources:
+      - "main:docs/adr/035-agents-work-only-from-memory.md (Affects line; section 1 Phoenix memory gets everything)"
+      - "main:.garura/project/issues/653/specs/plan.md item 3 (seeded from everything in core/components/memory/)"
+
+findings:
+  - id: MEM-01
+    severity: P3
+    file: core/components/memory/standards/schemas/product-os/ontology.md
+    line: 138
+    what: "The Source worked example now reads '**Given by:** the owner'."
+    principle: G3, G4
+    basis: >
+      The same file defines Given by as 'The git user who ran the reading play' (required, line 117 on main), and the /intent play
+      refuses an empty value because it 'must be the git user running the play' (capture_source.py:107-108 on main). The example no
+      longer matches what the play writes, and the product-ontology template asks for a real, filled-in instance as the reader
+      will see it (templates/product-ontology.md:60-62 on main).
+    fix: "Use a visibly fictional git-user name in the example instead of a role label."
+
+  - id: MEM-02
+    severity: P3
+    file: core/components/memory/standards/templates/adr.md
+    line: 154
+    what: "The gold example header now reads '**Decided by:** the owner'."
+    principle: G3
+    basis: >
+      The template's own field is '**Decided by:** <person>' (line 70) and accepted_by is 'The PERSON who accepted it' (line 28);
+      adr.py requires an accepted ADR to name who accepted it (147-148). The lint only tests that the field is non-empty, so the
+      example passes it while teaching a role label where the template wants a person. The committed ADRs 035 and 036 name a person.
+    fix: "Use a fictional person name in the example."
+
+  - id: MEM-03
+    severity: P3
+    file: "core/components/memory/knowledge/domains/personalization.md:51; knowledge/domains/payments.md:12; knowledge/technology/delivery-triangle.md:15; standards/rules/no-unbacked-recommendation.md:7 (and the two sites in MEM-01 and MEM-02)"
+    what: >
+      'The owner' is a new term used at six sites. Three of them start a sentence or paragraph in lower case (payments.md:12 after
+      a bold lead-in, personalization.md:51, no-unbacked-recommendation.md:7). The same removal is worded four other ways
+      (experience.md:86 'drafted on delegation', personalization.md:158 'per delegation', measure.yaml:20 'the PRIMARY frame',
+      delivery-triangle.md:15 'the owner's frame').
+    principle: G5
+    basis: >
+      Main holds no use of 'owner' for the person who decides or gave an answer. It uses 'owner' for other, defined things:
+      the quality-gate field 'owner: machine | human' (lens/quality.md:15-17, quality-gates.yaml:21), a 'product owner'
+      (rules/epics.md:13) and a document owner (domains/collaboration.md:58). ADRs 035 and 036 say 'person'. Once Phoenix memory is
+      read from other projects, 'the owner' reads as the reader's own owner, not the person it replaced.
+    fix: "Drop the reference where the sentence stands without it, or use 'the person'; use one wording per kind of site; fix the lower-case starts."
+
+  - id: MEM-04
+    severity: P3
+    file: core/components/memory/standards/rules/no-unbacked-recommendation.md
+    line: 7
+    what: "The verbatim quote that gives the rule its authority is now headed 'the owner, 2026-09-08:'."
+    principle: G3, G5
+    basis: >
+      The committed decision records attribute quoted answers by name ('Kapil: "..."' throughout ADR 035 and 036), and the rule
+      itself says a recommendation must name the source that backs it. A dated quotation with no speaker cannot be traced, and
+      in another project's Garura memory it is attributed to that project's owner.
+    fix: "Either keep the quote with a real attribution, or state the rule without the quote (the rule stands on its own text)."
+
+  - id: MEM-05
+    severity: P3
+    file: core/components/memory/knowledge/domains/personalization.md
+    line: 159
+    what: "'Intents proposed by Claude per delegation -- pending his confirmation.' The pronoun has lost its antecedent."
+    principle: G1, G5
+    basis: >
+      On main the sentence read 'per Kapil's delegation -- pending his confirmation'. The scrub removed the name and left 'his',
+      so the open confirmation has no named confirmer. Line 51 of the same file now says 'the owner delegated the intent list',
+      so the file names the person two different ways. Only a person confirms (ADR 031, main), so the open confirmation should say whose.
+    fix: "Reword line 159 to match line 51, and say who must confirm, or drop the pending note if the intents are confirmed."
+
+  - id: MEM-06
+    severity: P3
+    kind: grounding_unavailable
+    file: "core/components/memory/** (41 changed paths)"
+    what: >
+      The rule this change applies, that memory carries no personal names, is not in any committed source on main: not in
+      _TEMPLATE.md, _DESIGN.md, knowledge-file.md, ADR 035 or ADR 036. A search of main for a policy on personal names or attribution
+      in memory found none.
+    principle: G7, G3
+    basis: >
+      ADR 035 says everything in core/components/memory/ seeds Phoenix memory; it neither allows nor forbids rewriting content at
+      the seed. The committed schemas also require a person in some fields (G3), so a blanket rule needs its exceptions written
+      down. The policy exists only in text this branch adds and in the maintainer's own notes outside the repo; neither counts as grounding.
+    fix: "Record the rule where memory rules live (a line in the template's Provenance section, or an ADR amendment), with the schema fields that keep a person."
+    note: "This is a gap, not a violation. The edits that remove a name from a provenance line conform to G1, see conformance_checks_passed."
+
+  - id: MEM-07
+    severity: P4
+    file: "core/components/memory/knowledge/technology/ux-accessibility-floor.md, ux-design-system-tokens.md, ux-forms-and-validation.md, ux-navigation-by-shape.md, ux-state-and-feedback.md (frontmatter and ## Provenance of each)"
+    what: "'accepted by Kapil' became 'reviewed and accepted'. The word 'reviewed' is new."
+    principle: G1
+    basis: >
+      Provenance records the work context that produced the knowledge (knowledge-file.md:86 on main). The base text of these five
+      files says only 'accepted by Kapil' (git show main:<path>), with no review step. The two files that did say
+      'reviewed and accepted by Kapil' (collaboration.md, payments.md) are unaffected.
+    fix: "Write 'accepted' to match the base, or drop the clause."
+
+  - id: MEM-08
+    severity: P4
+    file: core/components/memory/knowledge/technology/delivery-industry-frames-derived.md
+    line: 39
+    what: "'Position (Kapil): this is the moat' became 'Position: this is the moat', inside the ## Recommendation section."
+    principle: G2
+    basis: >
+      The shelf is a bank of empirical learnings, and the Rationale is the empirical why (_DESIGN.md:8, :26 on main). A stance with
+      no holder reads as a finding. The file's provenance still names a measurement interview (#434), so it stays weakly traceable.
+    fix: "Say whose position it is in neutral words ('the team's position'), or move it out of the Recommendation."
+
+  - id: MEM-09
+    severity: P4
+    file: core/components/memory/standards/schemas/product-os/ontology.md
+    line: "199-205 (Log)"
+    what: >
+      Five Log entries are edited in place, and no new Log line, version bump or 'updated' change records the edit. The v2 entry
+      (line 200) now reads '(approving /intent's intent)', a fragment with no approver and, unlike the other entries, no issue number.
+    principle: G6
+    basis: >
+      The ontology's maintenance rule is to bump version, set updated and add a dated line saying what changed and why
+      (build-product-ontology SKILL.md:99-100). On whether a Log entry must carry its decider: it need not. The committed
+      Log shape is 'what changed in the ontology, and why' (templates/product-ontology.md:71), and the v3 decisions are held in
+      ADR 031 on main ('Decided in #612 ... by Kapil'), so removing names from the entries conforms. What remains is the unlogged
+      edit and the v2 fragment.
+    fix: "Add one dated Log line for the edit; give the v2 entry a subject or an issue reference."
+
+conformance_checks_passed:
+  - check: "Frontmatter facets (id, title, conditions, evolve_when, selection keys) unchanged in all 36 knowledge files"
+    principle: G2
+    result: "Compared main to HEAD per file, excluding provenance: no difference. No selection key, Condition or evolve_when pointer changed."
+  - check: "Where the body changed, it changed in the provenance and a few asides only"
+    principle: G2
+    result: >
+      Of the changed lines, 38 sit under ## Provenance, 33 in frontmatter, 5 in the ontology Log. The rest are the lead-in notes in
+      collaboration.md and payments.md, the 'Intents ... proposed' notes in experience.md and personalization.md, one line in
+      delivery-triangle.md Topic, one bullet in delivery-industry-frames-derived.md Recommendation (MEM-08), the quote in
+      no-unbacked-recommendation.md, one comment in measure.yaml and two worked examples. No Conditions, Recommendation or Rationale prose changed beyond that.
+  - check: "Provenance still references an issue or work context"
+    principle: G1
+    result: >
+      No issue reference was lost in any of the 36 files. Four files carry no issue number (agent-derive-once-apply-many.md,
+      run-environments-canary-migrations.md, astro-content-sites.md, quick-prototype-html-first.md); all four lacked one on main
+      too, and the first two keep a dated session or checkpoint as work context.
+  - check: "Removing a name from a provenance line conforms to the template"
+    principle: G1
+    result: "The template's provenance names a source ('seeded | learned from {product / issue}'), never a person, so 'interview (#434 -- Kapil)' becoming 'interview (#434)' conforms."
+  - check: "No duplicate or contradicting learning introduced"
+    principle: G2
+    result: "No learning added or removed; cross-references untouched; every evolve_when target exists (also observed in categories.yaml)."
+  - check: "The three edits that are not an attribution scrub are harmless"
+    principle: G1
+    result: >
+      agent-derive-once-apply-many.md ('prototype at a working prototype' to 'from a working prototype'), astro-content-sites.md
+      ('preferred' to 'the preferred') and builder-isolation.md (the doubled 'the source article' removed; 'Originating issue: #388' kept)
+      fix wording only and keep the provenance's source.
+  - check: "Does the scrub touch fingerprinted citations?"
+    principle: G7
+    result: "A search of main found no citation of a shelf file by line number, so editing these lines breaks no recorded source."
+
+summary:
+  principles_grounded: 7
+  findings: {P1: 0, P2: 0, P3: 6, P4: 3}
